-- ---------- Paper 1 ----------
VALUES ('8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 'aws-saa-practice-1', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 1', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['IAM', 'VPC', 'S3', 'RDS', 'EC2', 'Auto Scaling', 'ELB', 'CloudFront', 'Cost Optimization']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 1, 30);

-- ── Domain: Design Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('642cbb9c-f1a4-4aee-a766-be61ff8f6a46', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 1, 'An IAM user has an identity-based policy that ALLOWS s3:GetObject on a specific S3 bucket. A separate policy attached to the same user explicitly DENIES s3:GetObject on all S3 resources. What happens when the user attempts to read an object from that bucket?');
VALUES ('642cbb9c-f1a4-4aee-a766-be61ff8f6a46', 'c3f105ab-d858-4796-9338-7cfe495e99a0', 'IAM policy evaluation logic is: implicit deny by default, then any explicit Allow grants access, but an explicit Deny anywhere in the evaluated policies (identity-based, resource-based, permissions boundary, SCP, or session policy) always wins over any Allow, no matter how specific the Allow is or which policy is more targeted. This is a foundational, frequently-tested SAA concept — specificity and attachment order never override an explicit Deny.');

VALUES ('3701b31a-2d76-458d-b08b-bb98fa9fd7c9', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 2, 'A solutions architect needs to control inbound and outbound traffic at both the instance level and the subnet level within a VPC. Which statement correctly distinguishes Security Groups from Network ACLs (NACLs)?');
VALUES ('3701b31a-2d76-458d-b08b-bb98fa9fd7c9', '512dc327-4901-4078-bf3f-b1a651200bc0', 'Security Groups act as a stateful, instance-level (technically ENI-level) firewall — Security Groups only support Allow rules, and because they are stateful, a response to an allowed inbound request is automatically allowed out without needing a matching outbound rule. NACLs act as a stateless, subnet-level firewall, evaluated in numbered rule order, and support both explicit Allow and explicit Deny rules — because they are stateless, an administrator must configure both the inbound AND the corresponding outbound rule for two-way traffic to work, which is the single most common NACL misconfiguration in practice.');

VALUES ('669fbc81-8c0c-4baf-a476-d82875f22169', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 3, 'A company must encrypt objects in S3 at rest and requires full control over the encryption key''s rotation policy and access permissions, including the ability to audit every use of the key via CloudTrail. Which S3 encryption option should they use?');
VALUES ('669fbc81-8c0c-4baf-a476-d82875f22169', '8b508bad-634e-47f9-a335-6c5c494b1fe2', 'SSE-KMS with a customer-managed CMK is the correct choice specifically because it gives the customer control over the key''s rotation policy and IAM/key-policy-based access permissions, and every use of a KMS key (encrypt, decrypt, re-encrypt) is logged to CloudTrail as a distinct API call — this audit trail is the key differentiator. SSE-S3 uses AWS-owned keys with no customer control over rotation or granular access, and does not provide this level of per-use auditability. SSE-C (server-side encryption with customer-provided keys) is a third real option not listed here, where the customer supplies the key on every request; it was intentionally omitted from these options since it doesn''t match "full control over rotation policy" as cleanly as a KMS customer-managed CMK does.');

VALUES ('bf35b9f6-afe3-49f2-b659-b260fdc8f3a3', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 4, 'An application running on EC2 instances in a private subnet needs to read and write objects in S3 without traversing the public internet, and without requiring a NAT Gateway. What is the correct solution?');
VALUES ('bf35b9f6-afe3-49f2-b659-b260fdc8f3a3', '4f57ea09-ff0f-46c6-b9e7-72c1190099b6', 'A Gateway VPC Endpoint (one of two VPC endpoint types, alongside Interface endpoints) provides private connectivity specifically to S3 and DynamoDB by adding a route in the route table that directs traffic to the endpoint instead of over the internet — no NAT Gateway, NAT Instance, or Internet Gateway is required, and there is no data-transfer or per-hour endpoint charge for Gateway endpoints (unlike Interface endpoints, which use PrivateLink and do carry an hourly + data charge). This is a common SAA cost-optimization-meets-security scenario: it''s both more secure (no internet exposure) and cheaper (no NAT Gateway data-processing charges for this traffic) than the alternatives.');

VALUES ('c45a2a87-8ba4-4ad0-8af4-e42b6d90fec2', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 5, 'An application on an EC2 instance needs to call the S3 and DynamoDB APIs. What is the AWS-recommended way to grant this access, and why?');
VALUES ('c45a2a87-8ba4-4ad0-8af4-e42b6d90fec2', '22b3bb4a-1534-48f8-99c0-974abeb7e906', 'Attaching an IAM role to the EC2 instance (via an instance profile) is the AWS-recommended pattern precisely because it eliminates long-term credentials on the instance entirely — the instance retrieves short-lived, automatically-rotated temporary credentials from the instance metadata service (IMDS), removing the risk of leaked long-term access keys (a very common real-world breach vector: keys committed to a public repo, left in an AMI, etc.). Using a long-term IAM user access key on an instance, or worse, root credentials, is exactly the anti-pattern the SAA exam tests against.');

VALUES ('0768cacf-c4b2-47dd-b072-11ab497f453e', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 6, 'Account A owns an S3 bucket encrypted with a KMS customer-managed key that Account A also owns. Account B needs to be granted permission to decrypt objects in that bucket. What must be true for Account B''s access to actually work?');
VALUES ('0768cacf-c4b2-47dd-b072-11ab497f453e', '78b5bdce-7b25-4ae3-ade4-73eb52111a18', 'Reading a KMS-encrypted S3 object across accounts requires permission at two independent layers: the S3 bucket policy (or object ACL) must grant Account B the s3:GetObject permission, AND the KMS key policy must separately grant Account B (or a role in Account B) kms:Decrypt permission on that specific CMK. Missing either one results in access denied — this two-layer requirement is a frequently-tested SAA trap, since it''s easy to correctly configure S3 permissions and forget the KMS key policy also needs updating for cross-account scenarios specifically.');

-- ── Domain: Design Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('2eced134-e261-437b-a889-ef36956f6306', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 7, 'An RDS database is configured for Multi-AZ deployment. The primary database instance in AZ-1 fails. What happens?');
VALUES ('2eced134-e261-437b-a889-ef36956f6306', '0b1ea175-b528-42df-bc4f-59fc62a1803f', 'RDS Multi-AZ failover is automatic — AWS detects the primary failure, promotes the standby in the second AZ, and updates the DNS CNAME record for the database endpoint to point to the newly-promoted instance. The application keeps using the same endpoint address and only experiences a brief connection interruption during the failover (typically under a couple of minutes), not a manual reconfiguration. This is the core distinction the SAA exam tests: Multi-AZ is for high availability/failover, NOT for read scaling — that''s what Read Replicas are for (covered separately).');

VALUES ('3183ce08-850a-4fa7-be5a-af78c956a61d', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 8, 'An Auto Scaling Group is configured behind an Application Load Balancer with ELB health checks enabled. An instance in the group starts failing its health checks, but the instance''s own status checks (EC2 system/instance status) still show healthy. What does the Auto Scaling Group do?');
VALUES ('3183ce08-850a-4fa7-be5a-af78c956a61d', '568aea3c-521a-4351-9c7a-0ff90a4ff98f', 'When ELB health checks are explicitly enabled on an Auto Scaling Group (in addition to the default EC2 status checks), the ASG uses the ELB''s application-level health check result — which can catch problems the underlying EC2 instance status checks cannot see, such as the web server process crashing while the OS/network layer is still fine. An instance failing ELB health checks is marked unhealthy and the ASG terminates it and launches a replacement to maintain the desired capacity, independent of what the basic EC2 status checks report.');

VALUES ('698effad-9e9d-4302-ad8c-69128716a07c', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 9, 'A company needs its S3 data to survive the accidental deletion of objects AND to be automatically copied to a bucket in a different AWS Region for disaster recovery. Which combination of S3 features achieves both requirements?');
VALUES ('698effad-9e9d-4302-ad8c-69128716a07c', 'f2b06e57-194e-4e01-b24a-2b1b75320028', 'S3 Versioning protects against accidental deletion or overwrite by keeping every previous version of an object retrievable (a delete becomes a "delete marker," not a permanent removal, and can be undone). Cross-Region Replication (CRR) automatically and asynchronously copies objects to a bucket in a different Region for disaster recovery — and CRR has a hard prerequisite that Versioning must be enabled on both the source and destination buckets, which is exactly why these two features are paired together in this scenario, not because either one alone does both jobs. Transfer Acceleration is an unrelated feature (it speeds up uploads over long distances via CloudFront edge locations) and does neither of the two things asked.');

VALUES ('e870a968-b5c7-467d-99bb-852dfb7496c2', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 10, 'A company is migrating a legacy application that requires raw TCP connection handling and needs to preserve the client''s source IP address at extremely high throughput with ultra-low latency. Which AWS load balancer type should they use?');
VALUES ('e870a968-b5c7-467d-99bb-852dfb7496c2', '95559157-7242-4270-ad36-33b522854e7e', 'Network Load Balancer (NLB) operates at Layer 4 (transport layer), handles raw TCP/UDP traffic, and is purpose-built for extreme performance — millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency — and it preserves the original client source IP address by default (ALB requires reading the X-Forwarded-For header instead, since it terminates the connection at Layer 7). ALB is the correct choice for HTTP/HTTPS content-based routing (path/host-based rules), not raw TCP performance. CLB is the legacy, oldest load balancer type (not newest) and is generally not recommended for new workloads. Gateway Load Balancer is for deploying and scaling third-party virtual network appliances (firewalls, IDS/IPS), not general HTTP routing.');

VALUES ('a45feae4-4d9c-4009-b5b6-a7b2857512b0', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 11, 'A read-heavy reporting workload is causing performance degradation on the primary RDS database, which also handles the application''s write traffic. What is the correct RDS feature to address this, and how does it differ from Multi-AZ?');
VALUES ('a45feae4-4d9c-4009-b5b6-a7b2857512b0', 'c28f3a6c-154f-4ab2-b8aa-fe2520f9556c', 'This is one of the most frequently tested SAA distinctions: Multi-AZ exists purely for high availability/failover — the standby is not directly queryable by the application during normal operation and only becomes active after a failover. Read Replicas are separate, independently queryable database instances kept in near-real-time sync via asynchronous replication, specifically intended to offload read-only traffic (like this reporting workload) from the primary, which remains solely responsible for writes. The two features solve genuinely different problems and are commonly used together — Multi-AZ for HA, Read Replicas for read scaling.');

-- ── Domain: Design High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('b4d78584-4526-464e-9f03-f7f582ac3a59', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 12, 'An application repeatedly runs the same expensive, frequently-read database query, causing high load on the RDS instance. Introducing which AWS service in front of the database would most directly reduce this repeated read load?');
VALUES ('b4d78584-4526-464e-9f03-f7f582ac3a59', '788f3058-5eee-476d-b88d-d0eecfe640f5', 'ElastiCache is purpose-built as an in-memory caching layer that sits between the application and the database — the application checks the cache first, and only queries the database on a cache miss, which is exactly the fix for a repeated, expensive, read-heavy query pattern. CloudFront caches HTTP content at edge locations for end users, not database query results, and isn''t positioned between an application and its database. Direct Connect provides a dedicated network link to AWS (a networking/hybrid-connectivity feature, not a caching one). Global Accelerator improves routing to Elastic IP/ALB endpoints over the AWS global network, also not a database-query caching solution.');

VALUES ('9d9f096b-002e-44ea-a527-7e637094462b', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 13, 'A media company serves static video files from an S3 bucket to users worldwide and wants to reduce latency for users far from the bucket''s Region, while also reducing direct load on the S3 bucket. What should they implement?');
VALUES ('9d9f096b-002e-44ea-a527-7e637094462b', '252c8dcc-4a80-4eab-836c-61c81bece139', 'CloudFront is AWS''s CDN, and using it in front of an S3 origin caches content at edge locations distributed globally — requests from users far from the bucket''s Region are served from a nearby edge cache instead of round-tripping to the origin bucket, directly reducing both latency and the number of requests hitting S3 itself. S3 Transfer Acceleration is a different feature (it speeds up uploads TO S3 over long distances via CloudFront''s edge network, not general content delivery to many downstream readers) and is not a CDN replacement. S3 has no manual "provisioned throughput" setting the way EBS does — it scales automatically. Manually copying buckets per Region is a real DIY pattern in some cases but is operationally far heavier than the built-in CloudFront solution and isn''t what''s being tested here.');

VALUES ('2ef23090-f60b-44a2-9058-38eaba50cf69', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 14, 'A workload requires an EBS volume with the highest possible IOPS and sub-millisecond latency for a mission-critical, latency-sensitive database, with cost being a secondary concern. Which EBS volume type is designed for this?');
VALUES ('2ef23090-f60b-44a2-9058-38eaba50cf69', '071d84a4-3f25-4a08-921f-f7c4f222773f', 'io2 Block Express is AWS''s highest-performance Provisioned IOPS SSD tier, purpose-built for the most demanding, latency-sensitive workloads (large-scale relational databases, SAP HANA, etc.) with sub-millisecond latency and the highest IOPS ceiling of any EBS volume type. st1 (Throughput Optimized HDD) is designed for high-throughput, sequential workloads like big data/log processing — not high-IOPS, latency-critical use — and sc1 (Cold HDD) is the lowest-cost, lowest-performance tier for infrequently accessed data. Matching volume type to actual workload access pattern (IOPS-sensitive vs. throughput-sensitive vs. infrequent-access) rather than assuming "faster is always the SSD option" is a core EBS concept on the SAA exam.');

VALUES ('32c0c107-4a03-435a-b688-0f58eb62755e', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 15, 'An application accesses a specific set of S3 objects daily and requires millisecond first-byte latency; those objects must never be moved to a slower storage tier automatically. Which S3 storage class is correct for this access pattern?');
VALUES ('32c0c107-4a03-435a-b688-0f58eb62755e', 'da9a604f-9022-46bd-8cf4-f28be15505ec', 'S3 Standard is the default, general-purpose storage class for frequently-accessed data, offering millisecond first-byte latency with no minimum storage duration and no automatic movement between tiers. Glacier classes are for archival, with retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours (not millisecond access) and are the opposite of what''s needed here. S3 Standard-IA is designed for infrequently accessed data (with a retrieval fee per GB) — daily access doesn''t fit that pattern well and would incur unnecessary retrieval charges. S3 Intelligent-Tiering actively monitors access patterns and DOES move objects between tiers automatically based on usage — the opposite of "never moved automatically," which rules it out here even though it also offers millisecond access for its frequent-access tier.');

VALUES ('5030560e-6c53-45fb-ba41-8636cdf12730', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 16, 'A machine-learning training workload is CPU/GPU-bound and needs to process large in-memory datasets as fast as possible; cost is secondary to raw compute throughput. Which EC2 instance family is purpose-built for this profile?');
VALUES ('5030560e-6c53-45fb-ba41-8636cdf12730', '254405c3-4e77-4c22-99e9-c71a601745c7', 'EC2 instance families are purpose-built for different workload profiles: C-family (Compute Optimized) is designed for high-performance, CPU-bound processing; P-family provides GPU acceleration specifically suited to ML training and other GPU-parallel workloads. T-family (burstable) is designed for workloads with occasional CPU spikes and a generally low average utilization — it is explicitly NOT suited to sustained heavy compute, since burst credits deplete and performance is throttled once they''re exhausted, the opposite of what a sustained ML training job needs. D-family targets high-density, sequential-throughput storage workloads (like data warehousing), not primarily compute — matching instance family to actual workload profile (compute-bound vs. memory-bound vs. storage-bound vs. burstable) is a recurring SAA theme.');

-- ── Domain: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('2ff70523-f073-4aa9-ae88-250273789e5e', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 17, 'A workload is fault-tolerant, flexible on when it runs, and can be interrupted with minimal impact (e.g., batch data processing that checkpoints progress). Which EC2 purchasing option offers the largest potential cost savings for this specific workload?');
VALUES ('2ff70523-f073-4aa9-ae88-250273789e5e', '6da6dde4-52f0-4a5a-93f4-fd687721f0d3', 'Spot Instances draw on AWS''s spare capacity at steep discounts (commonly up to ~90% off On-Demand pricing) in exchange for the tradeoff that AWS can reclaim the instance with a short (roughly two-minute) interruption notice — which is exactly why Spot is matched to fault-tolerant, interruption-tolerant, flexible-timing workloads like this one, and a poor fit for anything requiring guaranteed, continuous availability. Reserved Instances trade a 1- or 3-year commitment for a discount on steady-state, predictable workloads, but don''t match a flexible/interruptible batch job as well as Spot''s discount depth does. Dedicated Hosts are for compliance/licensing needs requiring a physical server dedicated to one customer, and are typically more expensive, not cheaper, than shared options.');

VALUES ('1e26fab5-d2d2-4251-afd1-2d5381c0607e', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 18, 'A company stores objects in S3 whose access patterns are unpredictable and change over time — some objects are accessed frequently for a while, then rarely, then sometimes frequently again, with no reliable pattern. They want to minimize storage cost automatically without manually managing lifecycle rules or risking retrieval fees on objects that suddenly become frequently accessed again. What is the best S3 storage class for this?');
VALUES ('1e26fab5-d2d2-4251-afd1-2d5381c0607e', '9ff20300-df6c-43c2-adca-eb8b25097405', 'S3 Intelligent-Tiering is designed specifically for unpredictable or changing access patterns — it monitors actual object access and automatically moves objects between a frequent-access tier and lower-cost infrequent/archive tiers, and critically, moving an object BACK to the frequent tier when it starts being accessed again does not incur a retrieval fee (unlike S3 Standard-IA, which charges a per-GB retrieval fee any time an infrequently-accessed object is read). Manually-configured lifecycle rules moving objects to Glacier on a fixed schedule ignore actual access patterns and risk expensive retrieval fees if a "cold" object suddenly needs frequent access again — exactly the scenario Intelligent-Tiering exists to solve automatically.');

VALUES ('69fbc281-1a4f-4bee-a766-be61ff8f6a47', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 19, 'A company runs a steady, predictable baseline of compute usage across multiple EC2 instance families and sizes, and even occasionally uses AWS Fargate and Lambda for parts of the same workload. They want a flexible commitment-based discount that applies across all of this compute usage, not tied to a specific instance family or Region. What should they use?');
VALUES ('69fbc281-1a4f-4bee-a766-be61ff8f6a47', '448a2aef-b05d-423c-afd8-2f4251ffa8a5', 'Compute Savings Plans are the correct fit specifically because the commitment is measured in $/hour of compute spend, not tied to a specific instance family, size, OS, tenancy, or Region — the discount automatically applies across EC2 (any family/size), AWS Fargate, and AWS Lambda usage, which directly matches this scenario''s mixed-service, flexible usage. Standard Reserved Instances, by contrast, are tied to a specific instance family and Region (with EC2 Instance Savings Plans being a similarly-flexible-but-EC2-only alternative) and do not extend to Fargate/Lambda the way Compute Savings Plans do. Capacity Reservations reserve capacity in a specific AZ but do not themselves provide a discount — they''re about guaranteeing availability, not saving money, and are often paired with an RI or Savings Plan rather than being a discount mechanism on their own. Spot pricing is a separate purchasing model for EC2 specifically and does not apply to Lambda/Fargate at all.');

VALUES ('e9b12f34-2c56-4a89-b7d3-8f4e6a9c1d02', '8f0a1c23-3d76-4ea9-af5d-aac648412e1b', 20, 'A company has several NAT Gateways running in multiple Availability Zones, each incurring an hourly charge plus a per-GB data processing charge, even during off-peak hours when very little outbound traffic occurs. Which action would reduce cost without compromising the private subnets'' ability to initiate outbound internet connections during business hours?');
VALUES ('e9b12f34-2c56-4a89-b7d3-8f4e6a9c1d02', '5b08b203-4a55-46c2-bea0-84c2d6276151', 'NAT Gateways are billed hourly per gateway plus per-GB of data processed through them, so the cost-optimization lever is twofold: reduce the number of NAT Gateways where the resulting AZ-redundancy trade-off is acceptable for the workload, and — importantly — route traffic to AWS services like S3/DynamoDB through VPC Endpoints instead of the NAT Gateway, since that traffic no longer counts toward the NAT Gateway''s data-processing charge at all. Deleting NAT Gateways entirely would break legitimate outbound internet access needed for things like software updates or third-party API calls, not just AWS-service traffic, which VPC Endpoints don''t cover. NAT Gateway is a managed AWS service with no "instance size" to configure — that option describes EC2, not NAT Gateway, which is intentionally not user-provisioned by size.');
